course: dictionary
lesson: 3138
title: The ellipsis: three dots and missing words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An ellipsis is a mark made of three dots (…). It shows that something is left unsaid, or that a sentence trails off. «She opened the door slowly…» The dots make the reader pause and wait to find out what happens next.
explain: In grammar, an ellipsis means words are left out because the reader can already work them out. «I can swim and so can Tom.» The word swim is left out of the second part, because we understand it from the first part.
explain: In a quote, three dots can show that words have been cut out. Writers use ellipses to build suspense in stories and to show a pause or an unfinished thought. When you see three dots, ask yourself: is the writer waiting, or has something been left out?
fact: An ellipsis is written as three dots: …
fact: An ellipsis can build suspense, so the reader waits to find out what happens.
fact: In grammar, an ellipsis is when words are left out because they are understood from the rest of the sentence.
fact: «An ellipsis (...) can make the reader pause and wait.»
fact: The plural of ellipsis is ellipses, so one ellipsis, two ellipses.
exercise: «The door creaked open…» What do the three dots at the end show? | A question has been asked | A missing full stop | The writer has finished the chapter | *The sentence trails off, so the reader waits
exercise: In «I can swim and so can Tom», which word is left out? | so | *swim | Tom | and
exercise: Which mark is an ellipsis? | A comma (,) | *Three dots (…) | A dash (–) | A question mark (?)
test: Why might a writer end a sentence with an ellipsis? | *To build suspense and make the reader wait | To ask the reader a direct question | To make the word before it louder | To show the chapter has ended
test: In a quote, what can three dots show? | *Words have been left out | The speaker is singing | The quote is a question | The writer is angry
exam: Which sentence shows a thought that trails off? | I went home. | Pass the salt, please. | Where is the library? | *I wasn't sure what to say...
